```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r11
diagnosis_text:
r11 sits at the boundary between an EIGRP domain (segment EE / 112.0.0.0/8, connecting to r12) and the OSPF backbone (segment B / 111.0.0.0/8, connecting to r1). It is configured to redistribute OSPF routes into EIGRP (so r12 receives backbone routes) and to redistribute connected routes into OSPF (so the backbone learns the EE and FF segments). However, r11's OSPF router block is missing "redistribute eigrp 100". Because of this omission, the 120.0.0.0/8 prefix (r12's LAN, where pc12 lives) — which r11 correctly learns from r12 via EIGRP — is never injected into OSPF. Every backbone router, including r1, therefore has a complete absence of any route to 120.0.0.0/8, confirmed by "ip route show | grep 120" on r1 returning empty output. Forward traffic from pc12 gets as far as r11 via r12's static default route (0.0.0.0/0 → 112.1.1.1), and r11 can forward it into the backbone, but return traffic is black-holed at the first backbone router. This explains why pc12 can reach r12 and r11 (both in the EIGRP domain) but times out on any backbone destination. Root cause: r11's OSPF process lacks "redistribute eigrp 100", leaving 120.0.0.0/8 invisible to the entire OSPF domain. Corrective fix: add "redistribute eigrp 100 metric-type 2" (or metric-type 1) under "router ospf" in r11's FRR configuration, then reload or apply via vtysh.
=== END ===
```